WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.740
Welcome to Environmental Intelligence, episode 35, for May 19, 2026. Your daily briefing on

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.980
environmental regulatory, science, and compliance developments that matter for Canadian professionals.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:19.960
US EPA proposes delays for certain PFAS regulations. A move Canadian practitioner should monitor for

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:26.520
CCME and provincial threshold alignment. The US EPA signaled flexibility on timelines for some

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.980
forever chemical rules, while partially rejecting Hawaii's regional haze plan.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.320
Separate reporting shows Germany is on track to miss its 2030 greenhouse gas reduction target.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.840
Canadian consultants working on cross-border sites or federal CEPA reporting should review these

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:49.580
developments for any downstream effects on domestic guidelines this week. The Trump administration

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:54.880
released a rule-making proposal that would permit delays for selected PFAS regulations.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.780
This follows earlier actions eliminating the electric vehicle tax credit and rolling back

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:06.920
fuel economy standards. The proposal site slowed electric vehicle adoption as justification for

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:12.780
loosening automobile air pollution standards as well. For Canadian practitioners, the development

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:20.240
matters because CCME and provincial PFAS guidance often reference US EPA analytical methods and

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:26.820
risk values. Any change in federal US enforcement timelines can affect data comparability on

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:35.940
binational projects. Firms currently using US EPA Method 1633 or Draft 533 for site assessments

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:42.060
should track whether detection limit expectations shift. Next steps include watching the formal

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.960
comment period and any parallel CCME technical review scheduled for later this year.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:54.060
On the regulatory side, the US EPA partially reject Hawaii's 2024 regional haze SIP.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:02:00.940
The agency blocked permanent closure of fuel oil units at Maui and Hawaii Island power plants.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:06.260
This keeps certain emissions sources operating under existing permits and may influence

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:11.420
how Canadian jurisdictions evaluate long-range transport contributions under CPA.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:18.380
After prior rollbacks of California waivers and fuel economy standards, the agency now proposes

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:24.420
further delays in adopting tighter automobile emission limits. Cross-border fleets and Canadian

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:30.040
importers should assess whether revised US testing protocols affect their own compliance modeling.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:35.900
Turning to the science, warming erodes climate connectivity for terrestrial vertebrates.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:44.640
Under SSP5-8.5, functional effectiveness of climate connectivity is projected to decline across

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:54.160
77% of global land area by 2061 through 2080. Canadian risk assessors using CCME ecological guidelines

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:00.000
should consider whether habitat isolation factors now require explicit inclusion in terrestrial

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.460
ecological risk assessments. Isotopic analysis identified previously unreported sources of methane

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:12.720
from fossil fuels in Asia. While the study is Asia-focused, the methods are directly transferable

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:18.620
to Canadian oil and gas site investigations where source attribution affects liability allocation

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:24.540
under provincial contaminated sites regimes. In industry developments, Northland Power

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:30.520
advances renewable projects on the TSX. The company's pipeline of wind and solar assets remains

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:36.320
in focus for investors. Environmental consultants supporting project permitting should note that

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.460
federal impact assessment act timelines and provincial renewable energy siding rules continue

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:47.960
to drive demand for baseline studies. Intensifying droughts may push tropical forests past recovery

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:53.800
thresholds. Prolonged dry conditions are reducing post drought resilience and raising the risk that

00:03:53.800 --> 00:04:00.460
some ecosystems shift from carbon sinks to sources. Practitioners modeling climate-adjusted

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:06.140
site closure criteria in western Canada should evaluate analogous drought stress factors for

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:13.680
boreal forest reclamation plans. Now, speaking of PFAS work, here is something that comes up repeatedly

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:20.560
in the field. You arrive at a former AFFF training area where three consecutive quarterly groundwater

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:28.720
events returned non-detects for PFOA and PFOS, at 0.01 micrograms per liter, yet the client's

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:34.580
risk assessment still flags potential liability. The science here is that method detection limits and

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:40.980
matrix interferences can mask low-level transport, especially when sampling occurs during high ground

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:51.300
water velocity periods. Under BCCR-SAR protocol for, and Ontario OREG 153-04, practitioners must

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:57.300
confirm that the laboratory's MDL is at least three times below the applicable standard before

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:04.100
treating the result as truly absent. The experience consultant notices that seasonal redox shifts

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:11.760
or sorption to iron oxides can temporarily sequester PFAS, only for concentrations to rebound once

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:18.060
conditions change. The most common mistake is accepting a single non-detect round as closure

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:23.780
evidence without requiring a second event under low flow conditions and documenting the exact

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:30.980
extraction and analysis SOP used. If you are working on cross-border PFAS projects, review the

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:37.540
EPA proposed delay language for any language that could affect CCME soil and groundwater screening

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:45.520
values. Update phase 2 ESA work plans to include explicit documentation of PFAS method detection limits

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:52.720
relative to provincial standards. Monitor the Canada Gazette for any parallel federal consultation on PFAS

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:58.940
that may open in the next quarter. Brief clients holding cross-border portfolios on the Hawaii

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:04.980
Regional Hays decision and its limited but possible precedent value for long-range transport

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:12.260
assessments. Mark your calendar for formal publication of the US EPA PFAS delay proposal in the

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:19.280
federal register and any associated comment deadline. Check Alberta Environment and Protected Areas for

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:26.460
updates on EPA contaminated hyphen sites guidance that may reference new PFAS analytical requirements.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:33.760
Review CCME technical committee schedules for any soil vapor or PFAS guideline revision notices

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:41.460
expected before end of Q2 2026. Confirm laboratory accreditation status for method 1633

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:49.140
or equivalent before scheduling summer 2026 PFAS sampling events. Tomorrow watch for any update on

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:56.040
the formal comment period for the US EPA PFAS proposal and its potential ripple effects on CCME

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:02.400
technical reviews. That's environmental intelligence for today. If this briefing is useful to your

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:08.220
practice, share it with a colleague and subscribe wherever you get your podcasts. We're back tomorrow

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:14.940
have a productive day. This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice, editorial selection and

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:16.120
analysis are my own.
